WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.700
This tutorial shows how to use sticks to
reconstruct and inspect

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.500
a campaign plan. Structured threat
intelligence identifies techniques

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.400
and relationships, while an executable
procedure also needs concrete

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.300
actions, parameters, and environment facts.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.500
Sticks makes these choices explicit and
checks candidates against

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.700
a declared catalog. We will run the command
line, inspect the planning

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.400
interface, export a result, and repeat the
same computation.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.500
Planning does not execute an attack.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.400
Start with the anonymous zip linked in the
paper.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.000
Open a terminal in the extracted directory
and run the workbench

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.700
command shown here. The launcher installs the
pinned dependencies

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.300
on first use. Open the local address it
prints.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.500
No model key is required.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:58.000
We will inspect the input, compute a result,
export it, and repeat

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.300
that same computation through the command
line.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.680
First, use the command line to reconstruct
campaign C0045 Shadow

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.180
Ray. The output directory keeps the
procedural plan, derived environment,

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.580
and admission result. In this run, 10 of 10
compiled steps have

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.880
an admitted realization. That result concerns
the available catalog

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.080
and the declared facts. It does not show that
these procedures

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.280
were executed or that they reproduced the
historical actor.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.580
Now, reconstruct campaign C0001. The same
pipeline admits one of

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.580
27 steps and leaves 26 unresolved.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.480
Read the unresolved techniques and the
reasons.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.480
Missing catalog support remains visible.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.580
The tool does not silently fill these steps
with plausible commands.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.980
This is why the campaign identifier, catalog
version, and full

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.780
result matter more than a success label
alone.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.780
In the workbench, select Shadow Ray from the
campaign list.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.780
The default filter shows campaigns with the
frozen inputs needed

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.580
for enumeration. Set explicit limits for
realizations, search states,

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:08.980
and candidates per step. These are
computational bounds, not claims

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.480
about how many procedures exist in the real
world.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.980
Select Enumerate Realizations to run the
existing planning engine.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.980
The summary reports 64 complete realizations,
but only one procedure

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.180
assignment, one behavior contract, and one
causal structure.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.780
The 64 schedules differ in the ordering of
actions whose declared

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.980
dependencies permit that variation. They are
not 64 independent

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.580
behaviors. The search stopped at the
realization ceiling, so Search

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.880
Exhausted is false. The visible operational
status remains not

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:44.180
run.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:49.020
Open steps, each row identifies a compiled
source step and its

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.320
technique, then separates raw candidates from
assignments participating

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.120
in complete realizations. The positions
column shows where that

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.220
step occurs across the return schedules.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.720
A candidate count alone is not a count of
executable alternatives.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.520
Use these rows to locate coverage gaps before
proposing additions

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.220
to the catalog.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:13.420
Open realizations to inspect a schedule, its
templates, assumptions,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.420
and fidelity ceiling. This example reports
inspired fidelity.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.320
The declared composition uses benign
laboratory implementations.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.420
The workbench is not certifying historical
reconstruction.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.920
Different orders must respect the same factor
dependencies.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:31.320
The exported result preserves the detailed
graph and reachability

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.320
record for inspection.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.020
The proposal queue identifies declared
procedure deficits and generation

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.820
targets. Here it creates 10 targets from the
requested variant

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.620
counts. Constructing this queue makes no
model call.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.920
A generated proposal still needs admission
under the same trusted

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.120
contract and an implementation witness before
it can enter the

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.520
operationally bound catalog. This view
prepares work.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.820
It does not certify newly generated behavior.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:00.120
Download the JSON. The interface shows the
exact command for the

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.220
selected campaign. Run the command line with
from result and choose

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.220
a new output file name.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.020
It reloads the bundled inputs, recomputes
through the same service,

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.220
and compares the complete workbench object
before writing anything.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.220
The additional comparison shown here confirms
that the actual downloaded

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.320
and recomputed JSON results are equal.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.220
Keep both files and the artifact version
together.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.320
Change the maximum realizations from 64 to 8.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.420
The old result disappears and its download is
unavailable.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.520
Enumerate again. The new result now reports
eight schedules under

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.320
the new bound. This prevents an export
computed for earlier settings

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.420
from being mistaken for the current request.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.520
The new digest identifies the new result.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:47.520
To use another sticks to bundle, enter its
campaign identifier

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.320
and choose the JSON file in the admission
panel.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.620
This example imports the pinned bundle.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.720
Inspect admitted and unresolved steps, then
download the result.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.820
Repeat the same input through the command
line and compare the

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.620
complete JSON. No campaign action is
executed.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.700
This tutorial covered compilation, admission
inspection, bounded

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:16.600
enumeration, and auditable export. A live
campaign requires the

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.600
separate laboratory workflow, provision,
readiness checks, dispatch,

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:25.900
independent observations, and teardown. A
planning certificate

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.700
cannot replace that evidence. Follow the
laboratory guide for the

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:33.500
supported environment and retain each run
result separately.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.500
This narration is synthetic.
